    "reply": "I added `tests/test_payments.py` with 14 standard-library `unittest` tests covering `line_total`, `order_total`, and `refund`. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` passes, and I didn't touch anything under `shop/`.\n\nThe tests cover:\n- **`line_total`:** multiplication, zero quantity, and float prices.\n- **`order_total`:** empty orders, single and multiple lines, tax applied after summing, rounding to cents, a hand-checked value (20 \u2192 21.65), and zero-quantity lines.\n- **`refund`:** partial, full, and zero refunds, plus float amounts.\n\nI didn't mark any test as an expected failure. The module has no input validation, so negative quantities, negative prices, or a refund larger than the total all produce numbers without an error. Whether that's a bug depends on what you want, so I left those cases untested rather than locking in behavior you may not intend. If you want them guarded, the module would need explicit checks, and I can add tests for those once you decide the rules.",
